### What are backward and forward compatible changes?

Backward compatible means a new producer's output is readable by old consumers. Forward compatible means an old producer's output is readable by new consumers.

```java
package com.bank.contract;

public class CompatibilityTypes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Backward compatible: new producer -> old consumer can read");
        System.out.println("  Adding optional field with default: old consumer ignores it");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Forward compatible: old producer -> new consumer can read");
        System.out.println("  New consumer sees default when field is absent");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("BREAKING changes (avoid):");
        System.out.println("  Removing a field");
        System.out.println("  Renaming a field");
        System.out.println("  Changing a field's type");
        System.out.println("  Making an optional field required");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Backward compatible: new producer -> old consumer can read
  Adding optional field with default: old consumer ignores it

Forward compatible: old producer -> new consumer can read
  New consumer sees default when field is absent

BREAKING changes (avoid):
  Removing, renaming, retyping, or requiring a field
```

### How does expand-and-contract work?

Add the new field first, let consumers migrate, then remove the old field. Never a flag-day migration.

```java
package com.bank.contract;

public class ExpandAndContract {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Phase 1 (EXPAND): add new field, keep old");
        System.out.println("  Event: {amount: 50000, amount_minor: 5000000}");
        System.out.println("  Old consumers read 'amount' (still there)");
        System.out.println("  New consumers read 'amount_minor' (new)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Phase 2 (MIGRATE): consumers switch to new field");
        System.out.println("  Verify: no consumer reads 'amount' anymore");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Phase 3 (CONTRACT): stop writing old field");
        System.out.println("  Event: {amount_minor: 5000000}");
        System.out.println("  Only safe AFTER all consumers migrated");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Phase 1 (EXPAND): add new field, keep old
Phase 2 (MIGRATE): consumers switch to new field
Phase 3 (CONTRACT): stop writing old field
```

### What are provider contract tests?

Each consumer publishes the shape it relies on. The provider's build runs those contracts.

```java
package com.bank.contract;

public class ContractTests {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Consumer-driven contract testing:");
        System.out.println("  Account-service consumer expects: {accountId, balance, holderName}");
        System.out.println("  Statement-service consumer expects: {accountId, balance, txnHistory}");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Provider's CI pipeline:");
        System.out.println("    1. Run account-service contract against current output");
        System.out.println("    2. Run statement-service contract against current output");
        System.out.println("    3. If provider removed 'balance' -> BOTH contracts FAIL");
        System.out.println("    4. Build blocked before deployment");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Turns 'we think it is safe' into 'the build proves it is safe'");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Consumer-driven contract testing:
  Provider removed 'balance' -> BOTH contracts FAIL -> build blocked
  Turns 'we think it is safe' into 'the build proves it is safe'
```

### What is a semantic break and why is it dangerous?

The structure is unchanged but the meaning of a field changes. Every structural test passes while every consumer is silently wrong.

```java
package com.bank.contract;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SemanticBreak {
    // Before: amount field is in RUPEES  (50000.00)
    // After:  amount field is in PAISE   (5000000)
    // JSON structure: IDENTICAL
    // Schema check: PASSES
    // Contract test (structural): PASSES
    // Consumer behavior: WRONG by factor of 100!

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal amountInRupees = new BigDecimal("50000.00");
        BigDecimal amountInPaise = new BigDecimal("5000000");

        System.out.println("SEMANTIC BREAK:");
        System.out.println("  Before: amount = " + amountInRupees + " (rupees)");
        System.out.println("  After:  amount = " + amountInPaise + " (paise)");
        System.out.println("  JSON looks the same: {\"amount\": <number>}");
        System.out.println("  Every structural test PASSES");
        System.out.println("  Every consumer reads 100x wrong value!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix: NEVER repurpose a field. Add a new field instead.");
        System.out.println("  {amount: 50000.00, amount_minor: 5000000, currency: INR}");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
SEMANTIC BREAK:
  Before: amount = 50000.00 (rupees)
  After:  amount = 5000000 (paise)
  JSON looks the same
  Every structural test PASSES
  Every consumer reads 100x wrong value!

Fix: NEVER repurpose a field. Add a new field instead.
```

### Why treat Kafka events as durable contracts?

A REST response is consumed immediately. A Kafka event can be replayed months later. Old events must still be parseable.

```java
package com.bank.contract;

public class KafkaDurableContract {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Kafka events are DURABLE:");
        System.out.println("  Event written January 2024");
        System.out.println("  Replayed in March 2024 by a new consumer");
        System.out.println("  Consumer must still parse the old format!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Schema registry (Confluent/Apicurio):");
        System.out.println("  Compatibility mode: BACKWARD");
        System.out.println("  New schema registered -> registry checks compatibility");
        System.out.println("  Breaking schema -> REJECTED at registration time");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka events are DURABLE:
  Event written January 2024
  Replayed in March 2024 by a new consumer
  Consumer must still parse the old format!

Schema registry (Confluent/Apicurio):
  Compatibility mode: BACKWARD
  Breaking schema -> REJECTED at registration time
```

### Why never publish internal JPA entities to Kafka?

An innocent column rename silently changes the public contract.

```java
package com.bank.contract;

public class NeverPublishInternalEntity {
    // WRONG: publish JPA entity directly
    // @Entity Account { String holderName; BigDecimal balance; }
    // kafkaTemplate.send("account-events", objectMapper.writeValueAsString(account));
    // Later: refactor holderName -> customerName -> BREAKS every consumer

    // CORRECT: explicit event class
    // record AccountCreatedEvent(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance) {}
    // Map entity -> event deliberately
    // Event class is governed as a public API

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: publish JPA entity -> column rename breaks consumers");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: map to explicit event class -> governed API");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: publish JPA entity -> column rename breaks consumers
CORRECT: map to explicit event class -> governed API
```

### How do you handle truly breaking changes?

Version the event type as a last resort. Like API versions, every live version is ongoing cost.

```java
package com.bank.contract;

public class EventVersioning {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Truly breaking change that cannot be made additive:");
        System.out.println("  1. Create new event type: TransferCompletedV2");
        System.out.println("  2. Publish BOTH V1 and V2 (dual-publish)");
        System.out.println("  3. Consumers migrate to V2 on their schedule");
        System.out.println("  4. Stop publishing V1 after all consumers migrated");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Cost: every live version is ongoing maintenance");
        System.out.println("  -> Version only as a last resort");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Truly breaking change:
  1. Create TransferCompletedV2
  2. Dual-publish V1 and V2
  3. Consumers migrate
  4. Stop V1

Cost: every live version is ongoing maintenance
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Keep events small and intention-revealing. Document contracts like code. Prefer immutable, append-only event design.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Additive changes only (new optional fields with defaults)
  [x] Deprecate before delete with concrete sunset date
  [x] Provider contract tests in CI
  [x] Never repurpose a field (semantic break defense)
  [x] Schema registry with backward compatibility mode
  [x] Explicit event classes, never internal entities
  [x] Version event types only as last resort
  [x] Encode units explicitly: {amount: 50000, currency: INR, scale: 2}
```
